{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Ataroth and Dibon.</b> They were from the land of Sihon king of the Amorite, and among them are places of idolatry (ע\"ז) where the Amorites worshipped, namely Nebo and Baal Meon, in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 46:1): ״כרע בל קרס נבו״. And Onkelos translated this verse: מכללתא ומלבשתא וכומרין ובי נמרי ובי חושבנא ובעיל דיבון וסיען ובי קבורא דמשה ובעון. He used the term בי for some of them, for thus is the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory in the Talmud, that they so call places of idolatry, such as בי נצרפי, בי אבידן, בי דודבי עבדא דבי נורא. And the children of Reuben, when they rebuilt these, called them by other names, and this is what is written (below, v. 38): מוסבות שם, for they changed their names to be called by another name, so that the name of idolatry would not be remembered. And therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory said in Berakhoth: A person should always complete his parashiyyoth with the congregation, twice Scripture and once Targum, even עטרות ודיבון. Scripture mentioned this verse because there are in it names of idolatry, lest you say: since the Targum is the explanation of the verse, we need not translate this verse because it contains a mention of idolatry (ע\"ז) — therefore the Sages came to say that even in this verse it is fitting to mention it, so as not to treat it lightly." "<b>And you must</b> reflect that there is still a hidden matter in the building of these cities and in their names, and this verse will explain in the lower realms and allude in the upper realms to the names of the supernal building, and you will find in this verse nine names corresponding to the nine ויאמר of Genesis, for the tenth has no place to be mentioned because it is naught, and therefore they needed to say that even בראשית is an utterance (מאמר). And in this verse there is an arousal toward the emanation of the utterances by which the world was created, which are divided into groups of three, and this is the secret of the Mishnah's order of shofar blasts, three sets of three each. And what the Masters of Truth brought us as proof from this verse — that a person should complete his parashiyyoth with the congregation, twice Scripture and once Targum — the intent is to triple the verse and read it three times, to reflect upon those three levels in the building that are divided into three groups of three, and even עטרות ודיבון, to demonstrate that even a verse indicating the names of cities, in which there does not seem to be such great need at first thought, you will find in it a great and fundamental matter upon which all depends, because the entire Torah is one bond and one building, and there is no difference between the portion of the chieftains of Esau and עטרות ודיבון and their like, and between the Ten Commandments, for all is from one root and from one source, and its waters go forth from the Sanctuary, and all were given from one Shepherd. And this is the explanation of the verse: עטרות ודיבון ויעזר ונמרה וחשבון ואלעלה ושבם ונבו ובעון. עטרות — עטרת is written. ודיבון — this is the river that goes out from Eden, which never ceases, in the language of (Psalms 78:20) ״הכה צור ויזובו מים״, and in it is an allusion to the seventy-two names; and דיבן is written. ויעזר — this is Hod, as it is written (Psalms 89:19) ״עזרנו אלהי ישענו״, concerning the glory of Your name, and it is written (Psalms 145:5) ״הדר כבוד הודך״, and it is the left leg, and therefore a person needs help (עזר) from it. ונמרה — this is Netzach, and it is a notarikon of ימין ה' רוממה, and it is the right leg, as it is said (Psalms 16:11) ״נעימות בימינך נצח״. וחשבון — this is Jacob, as it is written (Isaiah 49:3) ״ישראל אשר בך אתפאר״, and it is the name יהו\"ה in the numerical value of חשבו\"ן. ואלעלה — this is Isaac, for he is a perfect burnt-offering (עולה תמימה), and this is the sense of ואלעלה עולה לאל, and to allude to his attribute it is written with a ה\"א, for in this very parashah it is written אלעלא with an אל\"ף, and likewise it is alluded to elsewhere: ישחק with a שי\"ן, to allude to his attribute, which is the great fire. ושבם — this is Abraham, and it is a notarikon of אברם סבא. ונבו — this is Binah. ובעון — this is Ḥokhmah, and reversed its letters are נובע, and בעון is written in the sense of a flowing source (מקור נובע), and further in the sense of מעון מעונה, which is an allusion to Ḥokhmah; and from this you will find elsewhere in this very parashah that he again called it מעון, as it is written: ואת נבו ואת בעל מעון. And behold that these names indicate great and fundamental matters, though they appear incidental at first reflection." "<b>And Scripture needed</b> to allude to the emanation (אצילות) in the names of these cities, which are the portion of Sihon and Og, to teach us that they worshipped idolatry (ע\"ז) through cutting of plantings (קצוץ נטיעות), like the generation of the Dispersion who said (Genesis 11:4) ״ונעשה לנו שם״, and they were the descendants of Cain, and so he called the power that caused man to sin by cutting of plantings; and therefore the children of Gad and the children of Reuben, when they knew this, changed the names of the cities to other names and wished to settle in them and to worship the Holy One, blessed be He, there, and to unify Him, in place of the cutting (קצוץ) that they — the idolaters — practiced. And this is what they said: If we have found favor in Your eyes, let this land be given to Your servants for a possession — that is, since the ultimate intent in entering the Land is worship and unification, here too we can unify the Name; and if we cross the Jordan, peoples from the outskirts will hear of the Land — that it is a land of livestock — and they will want to build in it and will return to worshipping idolatry (ע\"ז) there through cutting of plantings (קצוץ נטיעות) as had been the custom there at first; therefore let this land be given to Your servants for a possession — we will be the cause to abolish idolatry (ע\"ז) and to unify the Name, blessed be He."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:37:51.280239698Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}